<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emma Goldman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1869">1869</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emma Goldman was a Russian-born anarchist revolutionary, political activist and writer who played a pivotal role in the development of anarchist political philosophy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> to a Lithuanian Jewish family, she immigrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1885 and lectured on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchism">anarchism</a>, women's rights and social issues; with Alexander Berkman she planned the 1892 attempt on the life of Henry Clay Frick. She founded the journal Mother Earth in 1906, was jailed in 1917 for conspiring against the draft and was deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in December 1919. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kronstadt-rebellion">Kronstadt rebellion</a> she denounced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and later supported the anarchist revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emma-goldman.jpg" alt="Emma Goldman" width="330" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Takuma Kajiwara · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emma_Goldman_seated.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Emma Goldman</b> and Errico Malatesta held violence to be a reaction to state violence, a necessary evil.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.9,23.93&amp;year=1915" aria-label="Kaunas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5076 286 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Emma Goldman"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5526 556h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5526 556h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.9,23.93&amp;year=1915">What was here in 1915? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
